Abstract Magnetic field generated by active neurons has recently been considered to determine location of neuronal activity directly with magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), but controversial results have been reported about detection of such small magnetic fields. In this study, multiple neuronal morphologies of rat tissue were modeled to investigate better estimation of MRI signal change produced by neuronal magnetic field (NMF). Ten pyramidal neurons from layer II to VI of rat somatosensory area with realistic morphology, biophysics, and neuronal density were modeled to simulate NMF of neuronal tissue, from which effects of NMF on MRI signals were obtained. Neuronal current MRI signals, which consist of relative magnitude signal change (RMSC) and phase signal change (PSC), were at least three and one orders of magnitude less than a tissue with single neuron type, respectively. Also, a reduction in voxel size could increase signal alterations. Furthermore, with selection of zenith angle of external main magnetic field related to tissue surface near to $90^{\circ}$ , RMSC could be maximized. This value for PSC would be $90^{\circ}$ for small voxel size and zero degree for large ones.
Keywords Neuronal current MRI $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ Somatosensory $\cdot$ Realistic $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ Voxel size
## 1 Introduction
Functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) is a functional imaging method used widely in brain mapping research, but it depends on hemodynamic response and position of vascular system. Direct detection of neuronal activity can increase temporal and spatial resolution. In recent years, measurement of neuronal magnetic field (NMF) by utilizing of MRI has been considered [1–8]. This method is named neuronal current MRI (ncMRI). The mechanism underlying ncMRI is based on neuron physiology. Neurotransmitters in synaptic cleft stimulate dendrites of post-synaptic neurons and generate post-synaptic currents. Neuronal currents propagate in the dendrites toward soma. Action potential (AP) would be generated in soma and is transmitted to next synaptic cleft by axon. Magnetic field generated by neuronal currents in dendrites and axons could locally change external magnetic field used in MRI. Although it could theoretically change phase and magnitude of MRI signal extracted from an involved voxel with respect to resting state of neurons [9–11], detection of such a small signal is under debate.
Phantom experiments have shown that the signal detection limits depend on pulse sequence [1], voxel selection, and stimulation timing [12]. Direct magnetic resonance detection by in vitro experiments on dissected snail ganglia [13], organotypic rat brain cultures [5], and median giant fiber of earthworm [14] has been reported. On the other hand, NMF of visual evoked response of dissected turtle brain [15] and octopus visual system [16] was too weak for direct detection with MRI. Also, controversial results have been reported on in vivo human studies [3, 4, 7, 8, 17–19].
Some researchers applied simple dipole models to estimate the MRI signal alterations produced by neuronal magnetic field [11, 20–22]. Such simplification of neuronal current and morphology decreases accuracy of calculation. In order to simulate realistic estimation of ncMRI signal, some groups used real morphology and biophysics of nerves [23–25, 28]. Blagoev et al. [24] used BioSENSE software to model a realistic morphology and biophysics of monkey pyramidal nerve. They estimated $1.6\%$ magnitude change and $1^{\circ}$ phase shift in MRI signal [24]. In a study by Cassara et al. [25], CA1 pyramidal cell of rat was modeled by NEURON software. They reported linear phase signal changes and nonlinear magnitude signal changes related to different neuronal densities [25]. Also, Cassara et al. [25] showed that the MRI signal changes vary with respect to the echo time and neuronal density. Their simulation results anticipated 50 lradian phase change and around 8 ppm magnitude change for $100~\mathrm{{ms}}$ echo time and 2083 neuron $/\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ in a voxel volume of $1.728~\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ [25]. In two previous works, in order to estimate MRI signal changes in a voxel, single neuron type was randomly positioned in the voxel, but cerebral cortex commonly has six layers with different neuronal densities, neuron types, and morphologies [26, 27]. To simulate a more realistic model of cerebral cortex, Luo et al. [23] investigated the effect of neuronal density on ncMRI signal. They used human neuron morphology. Their results indicated that MRI signal changes due to physiological evoke response are around $-1.4\times10^{-6}$ and $0.02^{\circ}$ , which is below current sensitivity of MRI devices, but they proposed that spontaneous activity is detectable in proper conditions [23]. The previous works on simulation of NMF with realistic morphology showed that it highly depends on morphology of neurons [23]. Therefore, realistic neuronal tissue simulation with just one type of morphology for all layers could overestimate the value of MRI signal changes. Recently, a group of researchers developed a realistic cortical column architecture for primary visual cortex of cats which was applied to neural current MRI [28]. They proposed that synchronized neural activity, such as epilepsy, could produce large phase change in order of 100 lradian. In this model, dendrites and axons consist of randomly generated straight cables between synapses and neuron bodies. The reduced morphology, however, could not consider complexity of dendrite branches which play a main role in calculation of NMF.
The major goal of present study was to investigate effects of heterogeneous morphology on neuronal current MRI signal of somatosensory cortex in rats. Two distinct neurons of somatosensory cortex for each of layers II to IV with realistic neuronal density were used to evaluate NMF of complex neuronal model of tissue. In order to estimate ncMRI signals in the tissue, the calculated NMF was employed. In addition, density of active neurons, as a key factor determining the amplitude of ncMRI signals, was investigated. The ncMRI signal changes were also explored with decreasing voxel volumes. Besides, direction of the external field was studied to reach maximum ncMRI signal changes.
## 2 Method
## 2.1 Morphological–Biophysical Properties of Neurons
Morphology of neurons in each of gray matter layers is different even for same type of neurons [27]. To investigate the effects of multiple morphologies on NMF of tissue, two morphologies for each of layers were selected (ten different morphologies). The current study was focused on neurons of somatosensory cortex in rats. Because main neuron type in cortex is pyramidal cells [29], the model was confined to this cell type. 3D digital models were downloaded from NeuroMorpho (www.neuromorpho.org) [27]. As shown in Fig. 1, they have diverse size, shape, and extension over space. In Table 1, morphological parameters are listed. A neuron named $N_{t}^{L}$ means that tth neuron in layer $L$ . The active [30] and passive [25] properties of neurons were based on others works. All neurons had the same biophysical factors. Bioelectric parameters of neurons were as follows: specific membrane resistance $=28\ \mathrm{k}\Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , intracellular membrane $=150\Omega\mathrm{cm}$ , and membrane capacitance $=1\upmu\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ [25]. The active membrane properties contained four mechanisms: sodium channels, two types of potassium channels (A-type and delay rectifier channels), and hyperpolarization-activated cation current [30]. To model stimulation of neurons, randomly positioned AMPA-like synapses are applied to $2/3$ of apical dendrites of each neuron (rise/fall time $=0.4/1~\mathrm{ms}$ ). The selected number of synapses helped to generate action potential. Activation of synapses was selected randomly from exponential distribution with a rate of about $40~\mathrm{Hz}$ . Peak conductance for each synapse was about $0.5~\mathrm{ns}$ , but it was considered six high-density synapses to elicit a spike [31]. Although in real neurons also contain inhibitory synapses, for our purpose, the excitatory synapses are sufficient to show common firing rate of neurons in cerebral cortex. In addition, the number of inhibitory synapses is much less than excitatory synapses, which means these types of synapses could not have large effects on NMF [26].
Each neuron with its realistic biophysical and morphology was modeled in NEURON simulator [32].
Compartmental model with backward fixed-step Euler method was used to calculate membrane voltage in each segment over time with time steps equal to $T_{\mathrm{step}}=25~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ , and then ohm law was applied to find current in the segments. The simulation was done for $50~\mathrm{ms}$ . In order to calculate firing rate, time interval of two consecutive peaks was averaged over simulation time $(50~\mathrm{ms})$ ). Firing rates in somas are listed in Table 1. A frequency about $30{\mathrm{-}}100~\mathrm{Hz}$ is regular frequency of neuronal activity [33].
## 2.2 Tissue Model
Cerebral cortex tissue commonly has six layers [26, 29]. The layers are named from I to VI with respect to skull. In layer I of cortex, there is no pyramidal cell [29], so its effects were disregarded on MRI signal. As shown in Fig. 2, neuronal densities and thickness of each layer have different values in the layers [26]. It is expected that layer I with the lowest neuron density and thickness would have small effects on neuronal magnetic field. It emphasizes that neglect of the first layer effect is correct. The modeled tissue had $2\times2~\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ cross section and $1.705~\mathrm{mm}$ depth from layer II to VI. Total number of neurons in the voxel was about 4 9 105.
In each layer, two different 3D digital morphologies of somatosensory pyramidal neurons of rat tissue were selected randomly; i.e., ten distinct morphologies were used in the simulated tissue. Somas of neurons were positioned on related layers at random. The neurons also were rotated randomly between 0 to $2\pi$ . The activated neurons generated neuronal magnetic field. The number of specific activated neurons was a fraction of total number of those specific neurons in specific layer which were activated synchronously. These activated neurons were effective in calculation of magnetic field in the tissue. Activated neurons ratio (ANR) was constant for all layers, which is defined as:
$$
{\begin{array}{r l}&{{\mathrm{ANR}}=({\mathrm{number~of~Activated~Neurons~in~Layer~}}i{\mathrm{th}})/}\\ &{\qquad{\mathrm{(number~of~Neurons~in~Layer~}}i{\mathrm{th)}}}\\ &{\qquad=({\mathrm{number~of~Activated~Neurons~in~all~Layers)/}}}\\ &{\qquad{\mathrm{(number~of~Neurons~in~all~Layers)}}}\end{array}}
$$
In order to investigate ncMRI signal changes with activated neuronal density, ANR varied from 0.5 to $5\%$ . Because of computational limits, ANR was confined to $5\%$ (neuronal density of about $2900\ \mathrm{mm}^{-3},$ ), which is equal to 19,839 active neurons in the volume of $2\times1.705\times2~\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ . For larger neuronal density, extrapolation was proposed (refer to discussion section).
## 2.3 Neuronal Magnetic Field Calculation
It was shown that intracellular current is only important source of the magnetic field which is generated by neurons [9, 34]. Membrane currents and extracellular currents were ignored in NMF calculation. Contribution of extracellular current on NMF is two orders of magnitude smaller than intracellular current, and the influences of membrane current on NMF are negligible [34].
To compute NMF of tissue, in the first step, NMF surrounding each neuron was calculated. Since magnetic field reduces by distance, a cubic volume was considered around each neuron whose dimensions were more than $20\%$ of the size of a neuron in each direction. NMF was calculated in the determined volume for each neuron (Table 1). Somas of neurons were positioned on center of the volume. NMF was expected to be small for outside of the volume which can be ignored. NMF was calculated at discrete points in the volume with ${5-}\upmu\mathrm{{m}}$ steps in three directions. To speed up calculation of NMF, temporal steps were sampled each $0.5~\mathrm{ms}$ . Time step is small in comparison with neural current variation. To calculate NMF at a point, effect of magnetic field from all segments of neuron in the desired point was taken into consideration. As shown in Fig. 3a, origin of coordinate system was put on soma of neuron for determined volume. The magnetic field induced by each segment in $\mathbf{P(x_{0},y_{0},z_{0})}$ point of the volume was calculated by Biot– Savart law as follows:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{B_{x}^{k}(t)=I\times(y_{0}^{\prime}-y_{k}^{\prime})}\\ &{B_{y}^{k}(t)=-T\times(x_{0}^{\prime}-x_{k}^{\prime})}\\ &{B_{z}^{k}(t)=0}\\ &{T=\frac{-\mu_{0}I_{k}(t)}{4\pi r_{0}^{2}}\left[\frac{z_{k+1}^{\prime}-z_{0}^{\prime}}{\sqrt{\left(z_{k+1}^{\prime}-z_{0}^{\prime}\right)^{2}+r_{0}^{\prime2}}}-\frac{z_{k}^{\prime}-z_{0}^{\prime}}{\sqrt{\left(z_{k}^{\prime}-z_{0}^{\prime}\right)^{2}+r_{0}^{\prime2}}}\right]}\\ &{r_{0}^{2}=\left(x_{k}^{\prime}-x_{0}^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(y_{k}^{\prime}-y_{0}^{\prime}\right)^{2}}\end{array}
$$
where all points are transferred to $(x^{\prime}-y^{\prime}-z^{\prime})$ reference coordinate system. $(x^{\prime}-y^{\prime}-z^{\prime})$ is coordinate system of segments in such a way that selected segment between $\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{x_{k}},$ ${\bf y_{k}},{\bf z_{k}})$ and $\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{x_{k+1}},\mathbf{y_{k+1}},\mathbf{z_{k+1}})$ points is parallel to $z^{\prime}$ axis (Fig. 3a). Components of magnetic field due to $k$ th segment in the new coordinate system are $B_{x^{\prime}}^{k}(t)$ and $B_{y^{\prime}}^{k}(t)$ . $I_{k}(t)$ is current at time $t$ which flows on kth compartment. Magnetic field of each segment was transferred to $\left(x{-}y{-}z\right)$ coordinate system (neuron coordinate system), and these vectors were summed to determine NMF of the neuron for single point of the volume:
$$
\vec{B}_{n}(x,y,z,t)=\sum_{k}\vec{B}_{k}(x,y,z,t)
$$
where $\mathbf{B_{k}}$ is NMF of $k$ th segment at time $t$ in coordinate system of neuron and ${\bf{B_{n}}}$ is total NMF of neuron. Equivalent
current dipole (ECD) was calculated by Murakami and Okada method [35] to estimate power of each neuron for induction of NMF and to compare with similar works:
$$
\overrightarrow{\mathrm{\Delta}\mathcal{Q}_{t}}=\sum_{k}I_{k,t}\overrightarrow{L_{k}}
$$
where $\bf{Q}_{t}$ is equivalent current dipole at time $t$ and $\mathbf{L_{k}}$ is length vector of kth compartment.
In order to calculate NMF in the tissue, neurons were randomly rotated and positioned in the voxel. Main axis of neurons was assumed perpendicular to tissue surface (Fig. 3b). Computed points of NMF in tissue were discrete points with ${5-}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ space steps. Magnetic field induced by all active neurons for discrete points in the tissue was calculated. If target point was inside the calculated volume of each neuron, NMF related to this neuron was included; otherwise, its effect was neglected. Effects of all neurons were summed to determine NMF for target point in the tissue:
$$
\overrightarrow{B}_{t}(\vec{r},t)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\vec{B}_{n}^{i}(\vec{r},t)
$$
where $\overrightarrow{B}_{t}(\vec{r},t)$ is magnetic field of tissue in point of $\vec{r}$ at time of $t$ and $N$ is total number of active neurons. $\vec{B}_{n}^{i}(\vec{r},t)$ is magnetic field from ith active neuron. Also, it must be noted that the effects of random rotation of neuron were considered before summation.
## 2.4 MRI Signal Change Calculation
Since magnetic field in tissue, which is caused by neuronal activity, is very small in comparison with external field $B_{0}$ , only a component of NMF which is parallel to external field can affect precession frequency of spins. It was called $B_{||}(r,t)$ . If external field was in direction of $(\theta,\omega)$ , where $\theta$ and $\omega$ are zenith angle and azimuth angle, respectively, as defined in Fig. 3b, $B_{||}(r,t)$ was assessed by Blagoev et al. [23]:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\boldsymbol{B}_{||}(\vec{r},t)=\sin(\theta)\cos(\omega)\boldsymbol{B}_{x}^{t}(\vec{r},t)+\cos(\theta)\boldsymbol{B}_{y}^{t}(\vec{r},t)}\\ &{\qquad+\sin(\theta)\sin(\omega)\boldsymbol{B}_{y}^{t}(\vec{r},t)}\end{array}
$$
where $B_{x}^{t},B_{y}^{t},$ and $B_{z}^{t}$ are components of NMF in tissue.
Transverse magnetization due to neuronal field is given by Heller et al. [36]:
$$
M_{+}(\vec{r},t)=M_{0+}(t)\exp[-i\Phi(\vec{r},t)]
$$
where $M_{0+}(t)$ and $\Phi(\vec{r},t)$ are transverse magnetization and additional phase, respectively. The additional phase is given by:
$$
\Phi(\vec{r},t)=\intop_{0}^{t}\gamma B_{||}(\vec{r},t^{\prime})\mathrm d t^{\prime}
$$
where $\gamma$ is proton gyromagnetic ratio. It can be shown that phase shift and magnitude variation of MRI signal for a voxel by volume $V$ at echo time, $T_{\mathrm{echo}}$ , and a gradient echo pulse sequence due to neuronal activity are estimated by Cassara et al. [25] and Heller et al. [36]:
$$
\varphi_{V}(T_{\mathrm{echo}})=\tan^{-1}\left({\frac{\int\sin{\Phi({\vec{r}},t)d^{3}{\vec{r}}}}{\int\cos{\Phi({\vec{r}},t)d^{3}{\vec{r}}}}}\right)\approx\langle\Phi({\vec{r}},T_{\mathrm{echo}})\rangle
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{S_{V}(T_{\mathrm{{echo}}})=S_{0}\sqrt{\left(\frac{\int\sin\Phi(\vec{r},t)d^{3}\vec{r}}{\int d^{3}\vec{r}}\right)^{2}+\left(\frac{\int\cos\Phi(\vec{r},t)d^{3}\vec{r}}{\int d^{3}\vec{r}}\right)^{2}}}\\ &{\approx S_{0}\left(1-\frac{\left\langle\Phi(\vec{r},T_{\mathrm{{echo}}})^{2}\right\rangle-\left\langle\Phi(\vec{r},T_{\mathrm{{echo}}})\right\rangle^{2}}{2}\right)}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{l}{\displaystyle{\delta_{V}\big(T_{\mathrm{echo}}\big)=\frac{\Delta S_{V}}{S_{0}}=\frac{S_{V}-S_{0}}{S_{0}}}}\\ {\displaystyle{=\frac{\langle\Phi\big(\vec{r},T_{\mathrm{echo}}\big)\rangle^{2}-\big\langle\Phi^{2}\big(\vec{r},T_{\mathrm{echo}}\big)\big\rangle}{2}}}\end{array}
$$
where $\varphi_{V}$ is phase signal change (PSC), $S_{V}$ is magnitude signal, and $\delta$ is relative magnitude signal change (RMSC). The $S_{0}$ stands for $t=0$ , when there is no neuronal activity. In this study, PSC and RMSC signals were defined as ncMRI signals. Determination of PSC and RMSC signals in $x,y$ , and $z$ directions means that external field $(B_{0})$ was considered in the relevant directions. Simpson method was applied to calculate integrals. As a typical echo time for functional MRI, all signal changes were calculated with $T_{\mathrm{echo}}=50~\mathrm{ms}$ .
## 3 Results
Membrane potentials of somas are shown in Fig. 4a. Simulation parameters were selected such that firing rates are about $23~\mathrm{Hz}$ for layer IV to $85~\mathrm{Hz}$ for layer II (Table 1).
Calculated ECD (Fig. 4b) shows that NMF has different powers in $x,y.$ , and $z$ directions. Major component of ECD for $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}^{5}$ neuron is parallel to main axis of neuron $\mathrm{\Delta}_{\mathrm{\mathcal{Y}}}$ axis) similar to other neurons (not shown). The maximum magnitude of ECD is $1.25~\mathrm{pAm}$ for $\mathbf{N}_{2}^{5}$ . Peak values of ECD change with morphology even in the same layer (Fig. 4c). Compared to previous studies, maximum ECD value of present simulation is ${<}1/4$ of human neuron model [23] and equal to rat hippocampus model [25].
Neural magnetic fields surrounding neurons have a complex temporal and spatial variation. It decreases rapidly with distance (Fig. 4d). It should be noted that it is possible to have great values of magnetic field very close to neuron branch, but the values can quickly drop. For example, z component of NMF is $0.27{\mathrm{~nT}}$ at (0, 10, $-5)\upmu\mathrm{m}$ which reduces to $0.05~\mathrm{{nT}}$ at $(5,10,-5)~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ for $\mathbf{N}_{2}^{5}$ . The absolute value of magnetic field at boundaries of calculated volume is ${<}0.01~\mathrm{nT}$ for all neurons. It confirms that calculated volume of NMF for each neuron (1.2 times of neuron dimensions) is large enough to ignore NMF of out of the volume.
RMSC and PSC signals vary with active neuron density in tissue. These are illustrated in Fig. 5 for voxel with volume of $2\times2\times1.705~\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ . As it can be seen, the neuron density changes from 290 to 2908 neuron $/\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ $(\mathbf{ANR}=0.5\ –5\ \%)$ . Absolute value of RMSC signal in transverse plane $\scriptstyle(x-z$ plane) is $4.2\times10^{-10}$ $(0.4~\mathrm{ppb})$ for neuronal density of 2908 neuron $/\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ 1 $(\mathbf{ANR}=5\ \%$ ). PSC signal in y direction would be 1.77 lradian for the same neuronal density. Because of limited computational capacity, a linear extrapolation was used for large neuronal density (refer to discussion section). The figure shows that if activated density is $1.2\times10^{4}$ neurons $/\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ , components of RMSC and PSC signal would be about (1.8, 0.5, $1.8\ \mathrm{ppb})$ and $(-0.6$ lradian, 7.2 lradian, $-0.6$ lradian) for $(x,y,z)$ directions, respectively.
The authors studied effect of voxel volume reduction on signal change for neuronal density of 2908 neuron $/\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ (Fig. 6a, b). The results are valid for other neuronal density as well. A sub-voxel probed the volume to find maximum absolute value of RMSC and PSC signals (Fig. 3b). Subvoxels had constant length in $z$ direction (slice thick$\mathrm{ness}=2~\mathrm{mm},$ ) and quadrangular face in $x{-}y$ plane with a variable length of $10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ to $1.705~\mathrm{mm}$ . When the length is reduced down to $0.1~\mathrm{mm}$ or less, a considerable growth in maximum signal change is observed. For example, ratio of maximum RMSC in voxel lengths of $10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and $1.705~\mathrm{mm}$ is about 6.5. This value is 18.5 for PSC.
In order to find a layer with maximum signal change, relevant sub-voxel was considered and its y position was plotted against voxel size (Fig. 6a, b). Due to limited depth of layers, the centers of sub-voxels are always in layer $\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ for large voxel size (more than $1.2~\mathrm{mm}$ ). Maximum of RMSC signal almost is located in layer V, even in small voxel size. In most voxel sizes, maximum value of PSC was placed on layer III except for large voxel sizes as it was mentioned earlier.
Optimum direction of external field relative to main axis of tissue which achieved maximum MRI signal change is shown in Fig. 6c, d. As previously stated, a sub-voxel examined the simulated tissue to find maximum signal. Neuronal density was $2908\mathrm{\neuron/mm}^{3}$ . Zenith angle (h) for RMSC signal is about $90^{\circ}$ for different voxel lengths (Fig. 6c). This angle for PSC signal increases with decrease in voxel length from about $0^{\circ}-90^{\circ}$ (Fig. 6d). On the other hand, azimuth angle $(\omega)$ demonstrates large variations for all values of voxel length.
## 4 Discussion
Complexity of ECD and NMF for each neuron over time and space (Fig. 4) proposes that estimation of ncMRI signals with one-type neuron could not be correct.
Morphology has two effects on NMF. First, difference in biophysical properties of each neuron segment causes current variations in each segment. Second, number of segments could affect NMF value. For example, RMSC and PSC signals in a realistic simulation of hippocampus CA1 neurons in rats with maximum ECD 1.2 pAm were estimated to be about $1.3~\mathrm{ppm}$ and 25 lradian, respectively [25]. Echo time was $50~\mathrm{ms}$ , and neuronal density was $2083~\mathrm{mm}^{-3}$ . In present study, with same neuronal density, these values were about $0.25~\mathrm{ppb}$ and 2 lradian, but maximum of mean ECD was about $0.3\ \mathrm{pAm}$ . Laminar cortex model predicted a phase change of about $100\ \upmu\mathrm{ra\mathrm{-}}$ dian with $\mathrm{TE}=100~\mathrm{ms}$ and neuronal density of $37,000\ \mathrm{mm}^{-3}$ [28]. In our model, linear extrapolation of PSC curve has resulted in 22.3 lradian with $\mathrm{TE}=50~\mathrm{ms}$ and same neural density.
RMSC and PSC increase with size of neuronal density (Fig. 5). Uniform random rotation of neurons, which was used in this study, caused that RMSC and PSC signals have approximately same values for $x$ and $z$ direction. This isotropic rotation of neurons causes average value of additional phase in $x$ and $z$ direction to be small. This means that components of PSC signal have small values for $x$ and $z$ directions relative to $y$ . The isotropic rotation increases additional phase variation in $x{-}z$ plane, so $y$ component of RMSC is less than transverse ones because the RMSC signal depends on variation of additional phase (Eq. 10).
In comparison with study of CA1 hippocampus neurons in rats, current model has smaller values of RMSC and PSC, three and one orders of magnitude, respectively [25].
This difference could be related to dissimilar methodology, simulation parameters, and neuron morphology in both simulations. For example, in our simulation not only morphology of neurons are different (somatosensory neurons versus CA1 hippocampus neurons), but also multiple layers and densities were used for neurons (multilayer neocoretx versus single-layer CA1 hippocampus). These parameters could reduce mean and variance of additional phase in simulated volume for current model. With considering multiple layers, additional phase values in the volume would be more homogenous than single-layer model. Please note that RMSC is proportional to spatial variance of additional phase. Also, in our model maximum ECD signal is less than CA1 model, so mean of NMF was reduced. This could result in lower mean phase and PCS signal.
Linear extrapolation of PSC was assumed for large neuronal density. It seems that this assumption is at least valid for $y$ component of PSC which is axis of rotation, and there is no symmetrical phase to cancel each other. Because of phase change balance around the main axis, PSC in $x{-}z$ plane varies linearly and slowly with neuronal density. Some simulations with parallel, antiparallel, and branched dipole models suggested that PSC signal changes linearly [11, 20]. Also, it seems that RMSC signal increases linearly with simulated neuronal density. Cassara et al. [25] proposed that RMSC signal has exponential trend for large neuronal density because of strong increase in local field inhomogeneity. The results of dipole models proposed an exponential increase in relative signal change with ECD of 5 pAm for dipole density up to $10^{5}\mathrm{mm}^{-3}$ [20]. However, for low neuronal density linear fitting could be correct as proposed by the Luo [23]. As previously noted, linear extrapolation of presented model could assess the extent of PSC signal up to 37,000 neuron $/\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ although this result must be used carefully. The authors could not take consequence for extrapolation of RMSC signal in large neuronal density, so a linear extrapolation was preferred.
Previous study has shown that mean value of signal stays approximately constant as volume increases [25], but partial volume effect could decrease with reduction in voxel size, and therefore, it causes growth of ncMRI signal change (Fig. 6a, b). Signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) and temporal SNR (tSNR) of MRI signal depend on voxel volume [37], and these values decline with voxel volume reduction. On the other hand, contrast-to-noise ratio (CNR) is product of tSNR and the effect size [9, 38]. As a consequence, growth of effect size (PSC or RMSC) could compensate tSNR decrease with voxel volume drop. However, this should be further investigated.
In Eq. (7), part of NMF which is in the direction of external field was considered to calculate additional phase. In other words, change of external field direction regarding simulated voxel could alter ncMRI signal. PSC would be maximized if the external field is parallel to additional phase vector in selected voxel. But, the relation between external field direction and RMSC signal is more complex (Eq. 10). Simulations showed that maximum RMSC signal could be accessible for any volume and neuronal density when the external filed is approximately in transverse plane 1 $\scriptstyle\cdot-z$ plane) (Fig. 6c). It was expected because transverse component of RMSC signal is greater than the parallel orientation. This result is confirmed by other works [23– 25]. In the case of PSC signal, Luo et al. [23] suggested that higher signal was achieved using orthogonal external field, but Cassara et al. [25] proposed that there is no preferred direction of external field to get maximum PSC signal. Our simulation showed that when voxel size is reduced, zenith angle which maximizes PSC signal tends to $90^{\circ}$ (Fig. 6d). For large voxel size, coherent summation of the NMF in $y$ direction is more than in transverse plane. It causes zenith angle of maximum PSC signal to be near zero degree. In contrast, coherent summation in transverse plane dominates when voxel size in $x{-}y$ plane is reduced, and so maximum PSC signal would be in the $x{-}z$ plane. Human neuron simulation reported $90^{\circ}$ for zenith angle of maximum PSC signal with voxel size $1~\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ [23]. It seems that different morphology of simulated neurons makes this discrepancy. So, to achieve maximum signal, in typical voxel volume of fMRI (more than $2\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ ) zenith angle of external field for RMSC signal is $90^{\circ}$ and for PSC signal could be zero degree for rat [25], and $90^{\circ}$ for human and monkey neurons [23, 24]. Although azimuth angle of external field which maximizes ncMRI signals has specific value neither for RMSC signal nor for PSC signal as voxel size varies, our simulation showed that azimuth angle could have large effect on received signal for small voxel size, but it has moderate effect on large voxel (the results were not shown).
In this simulation, additional phase (Eq. 7) was evaluated for $\mathrm{TE}=50$ ms which is a typical echo time for functional MRI. Although the presented study did not investigate the behavior of PSC and RMSC signals versus time, it is obvious that these values are related to accumulation time according to Eq. (7). In some simulation studies, it was proposed that these signals rise with echo time [23, 25] while recent finding reports that phase signal oscillates with the phase accumulation time [28]. It must be taken into more study.
Detection of such small changes in magnitude and phase of MRI signal with current method and devices is a challenging procedure. In previous studies, to extract ncMRI signal with statistical analysis of rest–active magnitude images (RMSC), no significant MR signal change was observed [3, 4]. Our simulation also shows that RMSC signal is very small. On the other hand, PSC signal is in the order of some millidegrees. Cassara based on power analysis showed that such PSC signal required about 25,000 scans to be detected [25]. Our simulation proposed that PSC signal could be raised up to 20 times if voxel size is reduced. Theoretically, it is possible to detect phase changes with current MRI devices [23, 28]. However, BOLD signal and physiological noise are most important challenges to acquire functional images based on PSC signal [39].
## 5 Conclusion
In order to have more realistic neuronal tissue, a tissue was modeled with mixed-type neurons of rat somatosensory cortex for different layers and neuronal densities. The results showed that RMSC signal is three orders of magnitude less than previous findings for single-type neuron tissue, while PSC signal is one order less. In agreement with previous findings, it is another emphasis on dependency of ncMRI to morphology of neurons. Decreasing voxel size could increase peak of ncMRI signal change up to one order. If RMSC signal is acquired by ncMRI, the best zenith angle for external magnitude field is $90^{\circ}$ relative to main axis of neurons independent of voxel size, but in the case of PSC signal, external field varies from orthogonal to direction of neurons for small voxel size to parallel direction for large one. On the other hand, it seems that morphology could affect zenith angle of main magnetic field if PSC signal is target signal.
